    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#otto-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otto-i.jpg" alt="Otto I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#otto-i">Otto I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siegel_Otto_I_Posse.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Otto Posse, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lorraine">Lorraine</a> takes its name from Lotharingia, the middle kingdom created for Lothar II at the partition of the Carolingian empire in 855, which stretched from Frisia to the Jura. After passing between the West and East Frankish kings, it was attached to the German kingdom in 925 and governed as a duchy. In 959 Archbishop Bruno of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cologne">Cologne</a>, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#otto-i">Otto I</a> and duke of Lotharingia, divided it into Lower Lorraine in the north and Upper Lorraine along the Moselle and upper Meuse, entrusting the south to Frederick, count of Bar. Frederick’s house of Ardennes held Upper Lorraine for most of the next century, and Gothelo I briefly reunited both duchies between 1033 and 1044. After his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#godfrey-i-of-louvain">Godfrey the Bearded</a> rebelled against Emperor Henry III, the emperor gave Upper Lorraine in 1047 to Adalbert and, after Adalbert’s death in 1048, to his kinsman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gerard-of-alsace">Gerard of Alsace</a>. Gerard’s descendants ruled for almost seven centuries, while Lower Lorraine fragmented into Brabant, Limburg and other principalities and the name Lorraine came to mean the southern duchy alone. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bishop">bishops</a> of Metz, Toul and Verdun and the counts of Bar ruled substantial territories around it, and Nancy became the usual ducal residence from the twelfth century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lorraine">Lorraine</a> lay on the linguistic frontier of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>: French, in its Lorraine dialects, was spoken in most of the duchy, while the north-eastern <em>bailliage d’Allemagne</em> around Sarreguemines spoke German dialects. Most people were peasants growing grain and vines in the Moselle and Meuse valleys, with pasture and forest in the Vosges. The dukes drew much of their revenue from salt: brine springs at Dieuze, Château-Salins and Rosières supplied Lorraine and neighbouring lands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">Silver</a> mines in the Vosges, notably around Sainte-Marie-aux-Mines, and forest glassworks added to ducal wealth in the sixteenth century. The duchy sat on routes between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#low-countries">Low Countries</a>, the Rhineland, Champagne and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a>, and the fairs and pilgrimage of Saint-Nicolas-de-Port, where a great basilica was begun in 1481, drew merchants and pilgrims. Lorraine remained firmly Catholic: Charles III founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jesuits">Jesuit</a> University of Pont-à-Mousson in 1572, and the dukes repressed Protestantism. The duchy was also among the regions of Europe worst affected by witch trials around 1600, when the ducal prosecutor Nicolas Rémy claimed to have condemned some nine hundred people. The Thirty Years’ War brought armies, plague and famine that killed a large share of the population.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#rene-of-anjou"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rene-of-anjou.jpg" alt="René of Anjou" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#rene-of-anjou">René of Anjou</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Le_Roi_Ren%C3%A9.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nicolas Froment, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The dukes commanded modest forces drawn from their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassals</a> and towns, supplemented in the early modern period by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mercenary">mercenaries</a>, and their position between the French and German worlds exposed the duchy to every great conflict of the region. Duke Raoul was killed fighting for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> at the Battle of Crécy in 1346, and in 1431 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rene-of-anjou">René of Anjou</a>, who claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lorraine">Lorraine</a> through his wife Isabella, was defeated and captured at the Battle of Bulgnéville by his rival Antoine de Vaudémont with Burgundian help. The duchy’s greatest military moment came under René II, whose lands lay across the corridor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#burgundy">Burgundy</a> needed to link his territories. Charles occupied Nancy in 1475, but René retook it and, with Swiss and Alsatian troops, defeated and killed Charles before the city on 5 January 1477. In 1525 Duke Antoine destroyed a peasant army of the German Peasants’ War near Saverne in Alsace. During the Thirty Years’ War <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-iv">Charles IV</a> led a capable mercenary army in imperial and Spanish service, but France occupied the duchy from 1633 and razed the fortress of La Mothe after its siege in 1645. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>’s armies returned in 1670, and Leopold had to accept the demolition of Nancy’s fortifications when he recovered his duchy in 1697.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#charles-v-holy-roman-emperor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Charles_V,_Holy_Roman_Emperor,_seated_(1500%E2%80%931558),_formerly_attributed_to_Titian_(Alte_Pinakothek,_Munich).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Attributed to Titian, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The dukes were princes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, but the duchy’s ties to the Empire were loose and contested. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lorraine">Lorraine</a>’s own institutions included the Assizes of the old knighthood (<em>Assises de l’ancienne chevalerie</em>), a court in which the old noble families judged cases and checked ducal power, and Estates General that voted taxes until they ceased to meet after 1629. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> of 1542 between Duke Antoine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> recognised Lorraine as a free and non-incorporable duchy with only limited obligations to the Empire. The dukes also held the Duchy of Bar, united with Lorraine under René II after 1480, although the western Barrois beyond the Meuse had been held in fief of the French crown since 1301, which gave French kings a legal foothold. Charles III, raised at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#henry-ii-of-france">Henry II of France</a>, built a more centralised princely state: he extended Nancy with the planned Ville-Neuve from 1588, issued a general codification of Lorraine customary law in 1594 and enlarged the ducal bureaucracy. A younger son of René II founded the house of Guise in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a>, which led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#catholic-league">Catholic League</a> during the Wars of Religion and gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#scotland">Scotland</a> Mary of Guise, mother of Mary, Queen of Scots. Leopold restored orderly government after 1697 from his new palace at Lunéville.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#maria-theresa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maria-theresa.jpg" alt="Maria Theresa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kaiserin_Maria_Theresia_(HRR).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Martin van Meytens, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lorraine">Lorraine</a>’s most lasting legacy is dynastic. Francis Stephen, the last duke of the house, married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#austria">Austria</a> in 1736 and became Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francis-i">Francis I</a> in 1745; their descendants, the house of Habsburg-Lorraine, ruled Austria until 1918 and Tuscany until 1859. The Cross of Lorraine, adopted by René II, later became the emblem of the Free French under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a>. The Guise branch shaped the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#scotland">Scotland</a>. The duchy was also a remarkable artistic centre. The printmaker Jacques Callot of Nancy recorded the horrors of the Thirty Years’ War in <em>The Miseries and Misfortunes of War</em> (1633), Georges de La Tour worked at Lunéville, and the landscape painter Claude Lorrain, born at Chamagne, took his name from his homeland. Joan of Arc came from Domrémy on the borders of Lorraine and Champagne and visited Duke Charles II at Nancy in 1429. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#stanislaw-leszczynski">Stanisław Leszczyński</a> the architect Emmanuel Héré and the ironworker Jean Lamour created the Place Royale, now Place Stanislas, in 1752–1755, one of the finest urban ensembles of eighteenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>. The duchy’s position between France and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#germany">Germany</a> foreshadowed the later contest over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alsace-lorraine">Alsace-Lorraine</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#henry-ii-of-france"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-france.jpg" alt="Henry II of France" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#henry-ii-of-france">Henry II of France</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henry_II_of_France-Fran%C3%A7ois_Clouet_(altered).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Workshop of François Clouet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lorraine">Lorraine</a>’s independence was eroded by French expansion. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#henry-ii-of-france">Henry II of France</a> seized the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#three-bishoprics">Three Bishoprics</a> of Metz, Toul and Verdun in 1552, encircling the duchy with French garrisons. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-iv">Charles IV</a>’s alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and with Louis XIII’s rebellious brother Gaston of Orléans, who secretly married the duke’s sister Marguerite, provoked French invasion in 1633; the duchy was occupied for most of the next three decades and ravaged by war, plague and famine. The Treaty of Vincennes of 1661 returned it on harsh terms, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> occupied it again from 1670 until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-ryswick">Treaty of Ryswick</a> restored Duke Leopold in 1697. Leopold rebuilt the economy, but the duchy had become a pawn of great-power diplomacy. To end the War of the Polish Succession, France and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#austria">Austria</a> agreed in 1735–1738 that Francis Stephen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a>’s intended husband, would exchange Lorraine for the succession to the Grand Duchy of Tuscany, which he received in 1737. Lorraine and Bar went for life to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#stanislaw-leszczynski">Stanisław Leszczyński</a>, the deposed king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#poland">Poland</a> and father-in-law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louis-xv">Louis XV</a>, but by the secret Declaration of Meudon of 1736 he left the government to a French intendant, Antoine-Martin Chaumont de La Galaizière. When Stanisław died at Lunéville in February 1766, Lorraine was annexed to the Kingdom of France.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lorraine">Lorraine</a> shows how a frontier principality between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> survived for eight centuries, largely under one dynasty, defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a> and sustaining a brilliant court culture, before French pressure absorbed it and its last duke founded the house of Habsburg-Lorraine that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#austria">Austria</a>.</em></p>

